transformed-contravariant-coordinate-com.interpretation:
The symbol $x'^{\mu}$ denotes the $\mu$-th component of a position four-vector in a primed coordinate system, distinguishing it from the components $x^{
u}$ in the original, unprimed system. The index $\mu$ is a free index, typically running from 0 to 3 in four-dimensional spacetime, where $\mu=0$ corresponds to the time coordinate (often scaled by the speed of light, $ct$) and $\mu=1, 2, 3$ correspond to the spatial coordinates. The position of the index (superscript) signifies that this is a contravariant component. This means under a coordinate change from $x^{
u}$ to $x'^{\mu}$, the differential elements transform as $dx'^{\mu} =  rac{\partial x'^{\mu}}{\partial x^{
u}} dx^{
u}$. This transformation rule is the defining characteristic of a contravariant vector and is fundamental to the principle of general covariance.
